<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmad Shah Qajar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1930">1930</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmad Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> was the last shah of the Qajar dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, coming to the throne as a boy in 1909 after his father was deposed in the Constitutional Revolution. His weak reign saw foreign occupation during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Khan</a>'s coup of 1921. While he was in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, the Majlis deposed him in 1925 and made Reza Khan shah.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmad-shah-qajar.jpg" alt="Ahmad Shah Qajar" width="320" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bain News Service · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AhmadShahQajar2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ahmad Shah was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> on 21 January 1898, the seventh and final ruling member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar dynasty</a>. On 16 July 1909 rebels seeking to restore the 1906 Constitution overthrew his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mohammad-ali-shah-qajar">Mohammad Ali Shah</a>, and a Grand Majles of 500 delegates placed the eleven-year-old Ahmad on the throne. His uncle, Ali Reza Khan Azod al-Molk, governed as regent until Ahmad reached his majority and was formally crowned on 21 July 1914. The Grand Majles abolished class representation and created five new seats for minorities, two for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> and one each for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrians</a>. It also lowered the voting age from twenty-five to twenty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Ahmad Shah tried to repair the damage of his father's rule by appointing the best ministers he could find, but he was an ineffective ruler facing internal unrest and intrusion by the British and Russian empires. The Second Majlis met in November 1910 and achieved little, since the central government was too weak to carry out what it proposed. During the First World War Russian and British troops fought Ottoman forces in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. In 1917 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> used Iran as a springboard for an attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, and the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> responded by annexing parts of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and pressing the government for concessions.</p><p>By 1920 the government had lost virtually all power outside the capital, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a>, rebels and warlords controlled much of the countryside. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-persian-agreement">Anglo-Persian Agreement</a> and new political parties further immobilised the country, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> had to borrow from the Imperial Bank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Under that agreement Iran received only a small fraction of the income of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bp">Anglo-Persian Oil Company</a>. On 21 February 1921 Colonel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Khan</a>, Minister of War and commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-cossack-brigade">Persian Cossack Brigade</a>, carried out a nearly bloodless coup with three thousand men and eighteen machine guns and then became Prime Minister. He cancelled the Anglo-Persian Agreement and signed the Russo-Persian Treaty of Friendship.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1923 Ahmad Shah left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for reasons of health, and when the Majlis formally terminated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar dynasty</a>, proclaiming <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reza-shah">Reza Khan</a> shah as Reza Shah Pahlavi in 1925, the tour became an exile. He died of influenza at the American Hospital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1930 and was buried, under his will, in the family crypt at Karbala in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>. His brother, the former crown prince Mohammad Hassan Mirza, continued the dynasty through his descendants. Ahmad Shah had been married five times and had four children.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ahmad Shah Qajar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in Neuilly-sur-Seine, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.07,46.3&amp;year=1918" aria-label="Tabriz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5703 795 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ahmad Shah Qajar"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6153 1065h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6153 1065h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.07,46.3&amp;year=1918">What was here in 1918? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
